# Beachbot Codebase Standards

## Project Structure
- Configuration files and classes should be in the `config` module
- Testing follows pytest conventions with files named `*_unit_test.py`
- Examples should be placed in the `src/python/examples` directory
- URDF files should be stored in `env/urdf/`

## Robotics-Specific Patterns
- Use frames and coordinate systems consistently
- Position and orientation transformations are a core operation
- Implement proper transformation matrices for converting between frames
- Separate representation from computation
- Use consistent units throughout (SI units preferred)
- Robot configurations should be well-documented
- Joint types should be clearly defined (ROTARY, PRISMATIC, FIXED)
- Make heavy use of numpy for mathematical operations
- Use numpy.dot for matrix multiplication rather than manual multiplication
- Understand sign conventions for transformations, especially for rotations and translations
- Support special-case handling for frequently used transformations

## Frame Tree Implementation
- Frame trees should be composed of nodes (frames) and connections (joints)
- Robot kinematics should be represented using a tree structure with clear parent-child relationships
- Transform operations should account for the complete chain from source to target frame
- URDF parsing should handle all standard joint types and their properties
- Position and orientation transformations should preserve frame information
- Frame tree should be convertible to a kinematic chain for forward/inverse kinematics
- Consider frame relationships when transforming between coordinate systems
- Factory methods should be used to create standard robot configurations
- Frame lineage (path from a given frame to root) is essential for transformations
- Fixtures should be provided for common robot configurations (3DOF, 4DOF, 5DOF, etc.)
- All transformation operations must have corresponding unit tests
- Special handling may be needed for critical transform paths in the frame tree
- When transforming between frames, find the common ancestor to determine the transformation path
- Create utility methods for repetitive operations like getting joint transforms or frame lineage

## Code Quality Standards
- Ensure coordinate transformations maintain proper signs (positive/negative values)
- Pay special attention to rotation direction conventions in 3D space
- Position transformations must account for both translation and rotation components
- Orientation transformations must correctly handle reference frame changes
- Matrix operations should be validated for numerical stability
- Edge cases like same-frame transformations should return identity matrices
- Inverse transformations should be mathematically correct
- Test transformation chains with multiple frames to catch composition errors
- Verify transformations with known reference values for standard configurations
- Ensure joint limits are properly enforced during transformations
- Validate matrix determinants to confirm proper rotation matrices (orthonormal)
- Frame trees should handle cycles and invalid structures gracefully
- Test exact matching of expected values, not just approximations where precision matters
- Transformation matrix calculations should consider the correct order of operations
- When traversing up the tree, apply joint transforms directly; when traversing down, apply inverse transforms
- For numba-accelerated functions, ensure type signatures match the actual usage
- All variables in a class should be private unless used by another calling class.

## URDF Format Standards
- URDF files should follow the ROS URDF specification
- Each URDF file should define a complete robot with links and joints
- Links represent rigid bodies and should include visual and collision properties when relevant
- Joints connect links and must specify parent and child relationships
- Joint types should be one of: revolute, prismatic, fixed, continuous, floating, or planar
- Origin elements should define both xyz (translation) and rpy (rotation) attributes
- For rotary joints, axis of rotation must be specified and normalized
- Joint limits should include lower, upper, effort, and velocity attributes when applicable
- The robot should have a clear root link with no parent
- URDF files should be validated for structural integrity before use
- Visual representations should be simplified for testing and simulation
- Consider using XACRO for more complex, parameterized robot descriptions
- URDF files should be placed in the `env/urdf/` directory with descriptive filenames
- Reference URDF files by path relative to workspace root, not absolute paths

## Beachbot-Specific Conventions
- Configuration should be flexible, with robot parameters loaded from configuration files
- Frame tree implementations should support integration with existing kinematics systems
- Follow naming conventions specific to the Beachbot project (base_link, etc.)
- Prefer factory methods for creating standard robot configurations
- Standard robot arms (3DOF, 4DOF, 5DOF, 6DOF) should be available through fixtures
- Maintain separation between geometry and kinematics functionality
- Cartesian objects (Position, Orientation, Pose) should carry frame information
- Support for standard transformation operations between any frames in the tree
- Robot base frame should always be "base_link" by default
- All transformations must account for joint types and their current values
- Python files should be organized into logical modules (geometry, kinematics, config)
- Use consistent error handling patterns for frame and transformation errors
- Position and orientation transformations should be properly signed
- Follow established conventions for direction of joint rotation
- When implementing joint transforms, apply origin transform first, then joint motion
- Use specialized matrix creation functions from math_helpers module for consistent calculations
